WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.390
The idea that you're gonna do something
that you're not used to doing, simply

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:06.900
to develop your writing skills,
I don't think is that practical?

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:10.200
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here
again, and today I want to talk

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:13.470
about writing, writing in the
language that you are learning.

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:16.800
Remember, if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe.

00:00:17.095 --> 00:00:20.635
Click on the bell for notifications,
and if you follow me on a podcast

00:00:20.635 --> 00:00:22.375
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:22.855 --> 00:00:26.395
So I sometimes get asked like,
okay, Steve, you always talk

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:30.715
about input, reading, listening,
building up your vocabulary.

00:00:31.134 --> 00:00:35.125
Uh, and even you talk about the importance
of speaking and speaking a lot, at

00:00:35.125 --> 00:00:37.675
least once you have enough vocabulary.

00:00:37.855 --> 00:00:38.875
But what about writing?

00:00:38.875 --> 00:00:40.345
How do we improve our writing?

00:00:40.525 --> 00:00:47.310
And I recognize that writing can
be a way of improving your ability

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:51.420
in the language, a way of getting
to more output in the language.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:56.580
And for some people it's important
to their academic aspirations,

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:57.960
professional aspirations.

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:00.870
So what do I have to say
on the subject of writing?

00:01:01.379 --> 00:01:05.310
Well, first of all, I would
divide it into two sort of stages.

00:01:05.459 --> 00:01:06.000
Okay.

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:10.560
On the one hand, if you are
at an early stage in your, you

00:01:10.565 --> 00:01:14.440
know, learning of a particular
language, how do you use writing?

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:15.820
How do you get to writing?

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.620
And the second issue is if you are already
fairly proficient in the language, how

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:25.150
do you improve your ability to write,
you know, academic papers or professional

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:27.130
correspondence or things of that nature?

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:33.009
So first of all, with regard to using
writing at an early stage in learning

00:01:33.009 --> 00:01:38.235
the language, first of all, as I say
about all output activities, you have

00:01:38.235 --> 00:01:40.395
to have enough of the language in you.

00:01:40.515 --> 00:01:42.645
So you have to do a lot
of reading and listening.

00:01:43.485 --> 00:01:48.554
And as I mentioned many times in the
early stages, listen to what you read.

00:01:48.615 --> 00:01:49.725
Read what you listen to.

00:01:50.145 --> 00:01:54.044
Have the two of them sort of work
with each other to build up your

00:01:54.854 --> 00:01:56.095
familiarity with the language.

00:01:56.095 --> 00:01:59.634
You have to have that within
you so that you can then output.

00:02:00.274 --> 00:02:04.490
So when you start to output in the
language, writing actually has a number

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:06.470
of advantages compared to speaking.

00:02:06.919 --> 00:02:12.290
First of all, when you are speaking,
you tend to use words that you're

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:14.269
confident that you can use.

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:18.230
Uh, you're reluctant to use words where
you're not sure what the word means.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:21.950
You're reluctant to use structures
that you're not very confident

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:25.190
that you can really control
those structures, those patterns.

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:28.645
Whereas when you write you've
got all the time in the world.

00:02:28.795 --> 00:02:33.055
You can look up words, you can
look up, you know, the conjugation.

00:02:33.265 --> 00:02:36.595
You can look up different tenses,
tenses, different structures.

00:02:36.865 --> 00:02:41.845
So you can actually experiment using
words and, and phrases that you

00:02:41.845 --> 00:02:47.325
might not remember to use or feel
confident using when you're speaking.

00:02:47.625 --> 00:02:53.025
So writing is a great way to improve
your ability to output in the language.

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:58.605
Another thing I would say is develop the
habit of making your writing and speaking

00:02:58.605 --> 00:03:00.435
as close to each other as possible.

00:03:01.095 --> 00:03:04.845
What I mean by that is whenever I
have learned the language, I try

00:03:04.845 --> 00:03:08.475
not to have a very casual form of
the language and then have some kind

00:03:08.475 --> 00:03:13.615
of a more, uh, you know, stylized
form of, of writing in the language.

00:03:14.105 --> 00:03:17.070
When I go to write, it's a
continuation of my speaking.

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:22.740
So I, I wanna have my speaking as
logical and as neutral as possible

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:25.140
so that I can use that when I write.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:30.630
Even though I know that in languages
like Chinese, uh, French, there

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:34.350
is a stylized form of the written
language, but I don't use that.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:38.610
And I know that in every language there's
a very slangy form of the language.

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:39.660
I don't use that.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:44.715
I try to have a neutral form of the
language, that way when I speak, and

00:03:44.715 --> 00:03:49.065
as I develop my speaking ability, I'm
also developing my writing ability,

00:03:49.155 --> 00:03:54.435
and as I use my writing to improve
my ability to express myself, I can

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:56.265
bring that back into my speaking.

00:03:56.565 --> 00:04:00.885
So try to make your writing and speaking
as closely connected as possible.

00:04:01.215 --> 00:04:04.485
So all of these are ways to get to output.

00:04:05.955 --> 00:04:07.095
What should we write about?

00:04:07.245 --> 00:04:12.465
Obviously there are people who say,
well, you should keep a diary, but I

00:04:12.465 --> 00:04:17.865
have never kept a diary and therefore it
would be very unnatural for me to keep

00:04:17.865 --> 00:04:19.365
a diary in the language I'm learning.

00:04:19.365 --> 00:04:20.654
I'm unlikely to do it.

00:04:20.654 --> 00:04:23.085
I might start but I won't continue.

00:04:23.414 --> 00:04:26.534
So the idea that you're gonna do
something that you're not used to

00:04:26.534 --> 00:04:31.185
doing, simply to develop your writing
skills, I don't think is that practical.

00:04:31.424 --> 00:04:35.505
So if you can develop a correspondence
with someone where you're sending

00:04:35.505 --> 00:04:40.905
emails back and forth, uh, but
you're still trying to not use the

00:04:40.905 --> 00:04:46.849
sort of, you know, Twitteries or
"gonna" "wanna" type of English, Try

00:04:46.854 --> 00:04:51.679
to keep it fairly neutral, fairly
standard, and correspond with people.

00:04:51.679 --> 00:04:54.919
Then those are writing
exchanges that are meaningful.

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:59.280
If you like to keep a diary, by all
means, keep a diary, but it's not

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:00.990
personally something that I would do.

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:08.700
Now, once we move to sort of developing an
ability to write better for a professional

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:14.039
or academic purpose, you have to go
after the model of, of what you want

00:05:14.039 --> 00:05:15.390
to be able to do with your writing.

00:05:15.599 --> 00:05:19.030
So you have to find a lot of
material of the style that you want.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:24.870
Of the vo, which has the vocabulary
that you want to use and, and not just

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:29.260
the sort of technical vocabulary, but
also the way thoughts are introduced.

00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:34.364
You know, on the one hand, on the other
hand, Uh, there are ways that our thoughts

00:05:34.364 --> 00:05:40.125
are introduced in writing that can be
a little more formal than in speaking.

00:05:40.364 --> 00:05:44.255
So go find this kind of
content, import it into LingQ.

00:05:44.775 --> 00:05:47.895
I would even combine listening
with reading because when you hear

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:53.890
the same content spoken, it helps
to basically, you know, those

00:05:53.890 --> 00:05:56.469
turns of phrases into your brain.

00:05:56.979 --> 00:06:02.650
So as I said, uh, I'm not, I
haven't been active writing in

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:04.180
the languages that I'm learning.

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:09.039
Uh, and that has been the case throughout,
uh, including Persian right now.

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:14.200
However, uh, I was reminded that
there is a feature at LingQ which

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:16.960
helps people who do want to write.

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:18.940
So I thought I would have a look at that.

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:21.340
And show you how that works.

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:27.160
So if I go to the community page, there
is a thing called the writing exchange.

00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:34.540
So if I click on the writing exchange,
then I will see that people here

00:06:35.110 --> 00:06:42.200
have submitted, um, writing, which
the members are correct for them.

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:51.150
And, uh, so if we look at this, we can
see that, uh, in the case here of this

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:56.370
French one, someone has replied and
you'll see that someone has volunteered

00:06:56.370 --> 00:06:59.730
to, uh, correct the writing in French.

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:07.890
In fact, there were two different ... So
you can look at two different versions of,

00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:11.370
of the correction, all of which I think
for people who are interested in writing.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:13.140
This is quite good.

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:18.780
Here's one in Polish, and here again, this
person has corrected the Polish writing,

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:23.070
so I think that's a good thing to do.

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:27.180
You can correct other people's
writing in your native language.

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:34.580
The biggest question for me is what
am I gonna write about and, uh, I

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:38.715
think, uh, if, uh, there is sort
of genuine correspondence, then

00:07:38.715 --> 00:07:40.275
the writing becomes meaningful.

00:07:40.275 --> 00:07:45.674
Otherwise, if you, uh, are prepared
to, um, you know, keep, uh, a

00:07:45.674 --> 00:07:49.194
diary, then that would be a way
of, of, uh, getting you to write.

00:07:49.694 --> 00:07:50.395
Just curious.

00:07:50.414 --> 00:07:54.645
I haven't been here, but if I look
at, um, you know, my statistics,

00:07:54.974 --> 00:07:58.926
I hadn't realized this, but I can,
I can look at how I compared to

00:07:58.931 --> 00:08:00.224
taking a while to come up here.

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:02.400
How I compare.

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:06.930
So in terms of all time LingQs,
I am the second most active

00:08:06.930 --> 00:08:08.940
person in Persian on our website.

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:12.240
If I look at known words,
I'm in third place.

00:08:12.810 --> 00:08:14.880
Uh, that's for all time.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:19.840
However, if I look at the last, uh, three
months, then I'm well down the list.

00:08:20.670 --> 00:08:23.430
Uh, other people have
been more active than me.

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:24.210
I gotta be somewhere.

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:25.080
I'm number 10.

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:29.490
Anyway, one of the things I find
interesting in LingQ is that I don't

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:34.140
use all the features, but one of the
features that, uh, people could use who

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:38.490
are interested in writing and having
the writing corrected, and it is free, I

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:43.439
believe maybe there's an exchange of coins
and you just go to the writing exchange,

00:08:43.470 --> 00:08:45.420
which can be found at the community.

00:08:46.365 --> 00:08:54.855
So, uh, I hope that is useful and uh, if
I do get going on writing in Persian, Uh,

00:08:54.855 --> 00:08:59.865
I think I'll probably use the sort of,
uh, scrambled sentence or, uh, you know,

00:08:59.865 --> 00:09:06.525
integrated review functionality that is
now available on, uh, on, uh, iOS and

00:09:06.525 --> 00:09:11.895
will soon be available on all platforms
as a way of starting to compose to some

00:09:11.895 --> 00:09:14.355
extent in the language that I'm learning.

00:09:14.665 --> 00:09:22.615
So lots of reading, lots of listening and
then just, you know, try to, if you have

00:09:22.615 --> 00:09:25.945
to write professionally, then you will
have a real reason to write professionally

00:09:26.395 --> 00:09:31.135
and, and when you write, the, the other
issue that comes up beyond this question

00:09:31.135 --> 00:09:37.455
of, you know, vocabulary and phrasing
is, how do I structure my, you know,

00:09:37.455 --> 00:09:39.285
essay or whatever it is I have to write?

00:09:39.735 --> 00:09:44.265
And here again, what I would suggest
is in every language there are sort

00:09:44.265 --> 00:09:49.365
of manuals on how to write well, how
to organize your thoughts, essays

00:09:49.365 --> 00:09:51.255
for school or whatever it might be.

00:09:51.675 --> 00:09:56.625
And I would go and find those, uh,
manuals on how to write and how to

00:09:56.625 --> 00:09:59.205
express yourself in the target language.

00:09:59.565 --> 00:10:01.535
And then I would bring those into LingQ.

00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:07.890
Then I would learn from these so that
not only am I improving my language, but

00:10:07.890 --> 00:10:12.240
I'm also learning the different forms
of, you know, collecting your thoughts.

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:14.130
Uh, I studied in France.

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:18.795
In France, they have a very specific
way of organizing thoughts at

00:10:18.795 --> 00:10:23.715
Science Po, we had to have sort of
introduction, part one, part two.

00:10:23.925 --> 00:10:28.635
Part one had section A, B, part two had
section A, B, then there was a conclusion.

00:10:28.694 --> 00:10:30.105
So that was their format.

00:10:30.405 --> 00:10:34.064
But there are many different formats
and these formats can vary from

00:10:34.069 --> 00:10:35.755
culture or from language to language.

00:10:36.135 --> 00:10:40.395
So I would suggest that when you're at
that sort of a more sophisticated level

00:10:40.454 --> 00:10:45.335
and you wanna improve your writing, go and
find these manuals in the target language.

00:10:45.515 --> 00:10:50.345
Study them at lingQ, learn the words and
phrases, and along the way you'll learn

00:10:50.345 --> 00:10:54.875
how in those languages and cultures,
they want you to organize your thoughts.

00:10:55.025 --> 00:10:56.735
Okay, well, there you have it.

00:10:56.795 --> 00:11:02.130
I wanna leave you with two videos that
I did quite a few years ago also on the

00:11:02.130 --> 00:11:07.260
subject of writing, and I think you might
find that my views have changed a little

00:11:07.265 --> 00:11:10.860
bit or maybe they haven't changed so
much, but they might be useful reference.

00:11:11.190 --> 00:11:12.030
Thank you for listening.

